T240	Quotation 43643 44000	"Say what you like, there is a great deal in blood. His mother was a wonderful woman, most high-minded and intelligent. It was a pleasure to look at her good, candid, pure face; it was like the face of an angel. She drew splendidly, wrote verses, spoke five foreign languages, sang. . . . Poor thing! she died of consumption. The Kingdom of Heaven be hers."
T241	Cue 43626 43641	begin muttering
T242	Entity 43305 43321	Yegor Semyonitch
T243	Entity 43592 43604	the gardener
R314	Cueing Arg1:T241 Arg2:T240	
R315	Speaker Arg1:T242 Arg2:T240	
R316	Addressee Arg1:T243 Arg2:T240	
T244	Quotation 44064 44471	"When he was a boy and growing up in my house, he had the same angelic face, good and candid. The way he looks and talks and moves is as soft and elegant as his mother's. And his intellect! We were always struck with his intelligence. To be sure, it's not for nothing he's a Master of Arts! It's not for nothing! And wait a bit, Ivan Karlovitch, what will he be in ten years' time? He will be far above us!"
T245	Entity 44012 44028	Yegor Semyonitch
T246	Cue 44041 44062	after a pause went on
T247	Quotation 44604 44748	"The devils! They have spoilt everything! They have ruined everything! They have spoilt everything! The garden's done for, the garden's ruined!"
T248	Entity 44499 44515	Yegor Semyonitch
T249	Cue 44599 44602	cry
R317	Addressee Arg1:T243 Arg2:T247	
R318	Cueing Arg1:T249 Arg2:T247	
R319	Speaker Arg1:T248 Arg2:T247	
R320	Addressee Arg1:T243 Arg2:T244	
R321	Cueing Arg1:T246 Arg2:T244	
R322	Speaker Arg1:T245 Arg2:T244	
T250	Quotation 47271 47286	"Good-morning,"
T251	Cue 47287 47291	said
T252	Entity 47292 47300	the monk
T253	Entity 47326 47328	he
T254	Cue 47329 47334	asked
T255	Quotation 47336 47367	"What are you thinking of now?"
T256	Quotation 47368 47378	"Of fame,"
T257	Cue 47379 47387	answered
T258	Entity 47388 47394	Kovrin
T259	Quotation 47396 47584	"In the French novel I have just been reading, there is a description of a young savant, who does silly things and pines away through worrying about fame. I can't understand such anxiety."
T260	Quotation 47585 47707	"Because you are wise. Your attitude towards fame is one of indifference, as towards a toy which no longer interests you."
T261	Quotation 47708 47728	"Yes, that is true."
T262	Quotation 47729 48025	"Renown does not allure you now. What is there flattering, amusing, or edifying in their carving your name on a tombstone, then time rubbing off the inscription together with the gilding? Moreover, happily there are too many of you for the weak memory of mankind to be able to retain your names."
T263	Quotation 48026 48038	"Of course,"
T264	Cue 48039 48047	assented
T265	Entity 48048 48054	Kovrin
T266	Quotation 48056 48179	"Besides, why should they be remembered? But let us talk of something else. Of happiness, for instance. What is happiness?"
T267	Cue 48268 48275	talking
T268	Entity 48208 48210	he
T269	Entity 48279 48287	the monk
T270	Quotation 48289 48826	"In ancient times a happy man grew at last frightened of his happiness --it was so great!-- and to propitiate the gods he brought as a sacrifice his favourite ring. Do you know, I, too, like Polykrates, begin to be uneasy of my happiness. It seems strange to me that from morning to night I feel nothing but joy; it fills my whole being and smothers all other feelings. I don't know what sadness, grief, or boredom is. Here I am not asleep; I suffer from sleeplessness, but I am not dull. I say it in earnest; I begin to feel perplexed."
T271	Quotation 48827 48837	"But why?"
T272	Quotation 48864 49214	"Is joy a supernatural feeling? Ought it not to be the normal state of man? The more highly a man is developed on the intellectual and moral side, the more independent he is, the more pleasure life gives him. Socrates, Diogenes, and Marcus Aurelius, were joyful, not sorrowful. And the Apostle tells us: 'Rejoice continually'; 'Rejoice and be glad.'"
T273	Entity 48838 48846	the monk
T274	Cue 48847 48852	asked
T275	Cue 49264 49270	jested
T276	Entity 49257 49263	Kovrin
T277	Quotation 49215 49256	"But will the gods be suddenly wrathful?"
T278	Quotation 49288 49385	"If they take from me comfort and make me go cold and hungry, it won't be very much to my taste."
T279	Quotation 49603 49640	"Andryusha, whom are you talking to?"
T280	Entity 49641 49644	she
T281	Cue 49645 49650	asked
T282	Entity 49671 49673	he
T283	Quotation 49701 49719	"Andryusha! Whom?"
T284	Quotation 49720 49731	"Oh! Whom?"
T285	Cue 49732 49736	said
T286	Entity 49737 49743	Kovrin
T287	Quotation 49758 49798	"Why, to him. . . . He is sitting here,"
T288	Entity 49799 49801	he
T289	Quotation 49836 49896	"There is no one here . . . no one! Andryusha, you are ill!"
T290	Entity 49921 49932	her husband
T291	Quotation 50031 50045	"You are ill!"
T292	Entity 50046 50049	she
T293	Cue 50050 50056	sobbed
T294	Quotation 50078 50236	"Forgive me, my precious, my dear one, but I have noticed for a long time that your mind is clouded in some way. . . . You are mentally ill, Andryusha. . . ."
T295	Quotation 50411 50447	"It's nothing, Tanya; it's nothing,"
T296	Entity 50448 50450	he
T297	Cue 50451 50459	muttered
T298	Quotation 50472 50531	"I really am not quite well . . . it's time to admit that."
T299	Quotation 50532 50600	"I have noticed it for a long time . . . and father has noticed it,"
T300	Entity 50601 50604	she
T301	Cue 50605 50609	said
T302	Quotation 50640 50839	"You talk to yourself, smile somehow strangely . . . and can't sleep. Oh, my God, my God, save us!" she said in terror. "But don't be frightened, Andryusha; for God's sake don't be frightened. . . ."
T303	Quotation 51312 51345	"Don't be frightened, Andryusha,"
T304	Entity 51346 51351	Tanya
T305	Cue 51352 51362	was saying
T306	Quotation 51396 51489	"don't be frightened. . . . Father, it will all pass over . . . it will all pass over. . . ."
